In Java, the `==` operator is used to compare primitive data types (such as integers, booleans, and characters) for reference equality, i.e., whether they refer to the same object in memory. However, when it comes to comparing objects (such as Strings), the `==` operator checks whether the two objects refer to the same instance in memory, not their content. This means that comparing two String objects using `==` will only return true if they are the exact same object, not if they have the same sequence of characters.

On the other hand, the `.equals()` method is specifically designed for comparing the content of objects, including Strings. The `.equals()` method for Strings compares the characters in the two Strings, character by character, to determine if they are equal. This method is the preferred way to compare Strings in Java, as it correctly handles cases where the Strings have the same content but are not the same object (e.g., when using string literals or when creating new String objects).

In summary, you should use the `.equals()` method to compare Strings in Java, as it correctly compares their content, while the `==` operator should be used only for primitive data types or to compare references to the same object.

Example:

```java
String str1 = "Hello, world! ";
String str2 = "Hello, world! ";

// Using == operator to compare Strings
if (str1 == str2) {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 are the same object");
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 are different objects");
}

// Using .equals() method to compare Strings
if (str1.equals(str2)) {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 have the same content");
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 have different content");
}
```

